('reading', 'multiple_choice', 'Climate Science', 'hard', 7, 'What is a retrogressive thaw slump, as described in the passage?', '24000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', null, '["A gradual, predictable deepening of the active layer","A sudden collapse of a hillside exposing previously frozen soil","A new form of vegetation that grows on thawed ground","A specific type of infrastructure damage caused by pipelines"]', '"A sudden collapse of a hillside exposing previously frozen soil"', 'The passage describes it as an event "in which a section of previously stable hillside gives way entirely, exposing large volumes of previously frozen soil."', 'Do not confuse this with thermokarst, described in the same paragraph as ground subsidence rather than hillside collapse.', '{"multiple_choice"}', 70, 8, false),
('reading', 'multiple_choice', 'Climate Science', 'hard', 7, 'Why is the permafrost carbon feedback described as self-reinforcing?', '24000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000005', null, '["Because thawing releases gases that cause further warming, which drives further thaw","Because permafrost regenerates itself faster than it can thaw","Because the feedback only occurs during the winter months","Because it steadily reduces the total amount of atmospheric methane"]', '"Because thawing releases gases that cause further warming, which drives further thaw"', 'The passage describes the cycle: "warming causes permafrost to thaw, thawing releases greenhouse gases... and these additional emissions contribute to further warming, which in turn drives further thaw."', 'Trace the full causal chain described in paragraph 3 rather than selecting just one link in isolation.', '{"multiple_choice"}', 70, 9, false),
